part.text:
The `connection()` function from `next/server` is the recommended approach in Next.js 15+ for opting out of static prerendering when you need dynamic data without relying on request-specific data like cookies or headers.

I have created a server component `app/ServerTimestamp.tsx` that uses `await connection()` to ensure the timestamp is generated on every request.

### 1. Created `app/ServerTimestamp.tsx`
```tsx
import { connection } from 'next/server';

export default async function ServerTimestamp() {
  await connection();
  const timestamp = new Date().toISOString();

  return (
    <div>
      <h2>Server Timestamp</h2>
      <p>{timestamp}</p>
    </div>
  );
}
```

### 2. Updated `app/page.tsx`
I updated the main page to include the new component:
```tsx
import ServerTimestamp from './ServerTimestamp';

export default function Home() {
  return (
    <main>
      <h1>Dynamic Content</h1>
      <ServerTimestamp />
    </main>
  );
}
```

The application build confirms that the route is now **Dynamic (ƒ)**, meaning it will be server-rendered on demand as requested.
